{"kind":"assert","text":"Review findings for exact head 2d3b03a96f0112bbe62027e0f365e6076c3c16b7 on request/supersede-abandon, answering request #24527 under implementation promise #24528. Changes are required.\n\n1. [P2] Add a behavioral MCP abandonment test. cmd/gitseq-mcp/main_test.go@2d3b03a96f0112bbe62027e0f365e6076c3c16b7:3107 checks only the advertised schema. In a disposable clone I changed cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go:1442 to Abandon: call.Arguments[\"abandon\"] == true \u0026\u0026 false. This mutant compiles and the whole cmd/gitseq-mcp suite still passes (66.170s). The required red-proof test for this surface is missing. Exercise supersede through dispatch against a request holding an approved head, assert the signed schema and disposition and the abandoned projection, and demonstrate that dropping the dispatch argument makes it fail.\n\n2. [P2] Name --abandon in the CLI refusal. The request explicitly requires this. The existing fold reason remains \"request holds approved head ...; carry it in the successor or declare abandoned\", and cmd/gs/preflight.go supplies no matching repair hint. The focused test reproduces this output; the only named flag in the refusal is --no-preflight, which cannot make that retirement effective. Add a CLI repair hint for --abandon while preserving the fold's surface-independent reason, and assert it in the refusal test.\n\n3. [P2] Repair the carry command in docs/reference/gs/supersede.md@2d3b03a96f0112bbe62027e0f365e6076c3c16b7:46. \"gs supersede \u003cold\u003e --rests-on \u003csuccessor\u003e\" fails with \"supersede requires one target event\", because flag parsing stops at the positional target. It also omits required --text. Give an executable form with flags before the target, such as gs supersede --as \u003cactor\u003e --text \u003cwhy\u003e --rests-on \u003csuccessor\u003e \u003cold\u003e. The new abandonment command in SKILL.md:171 also needs the positional target.\n\n4. [P2] Update and publish docs/reference/architecture.md in the repair head. This head extends the CLI and MCP surface contracts with --abandon and abandon, and the layer-4 app adapter with Act.Abandon and its schema selection, but leaves the architecture page unchanged. The same-head architecture update and artifact are required for a contract change by that page's Review rule and SKILL.md.\n\nArchitecture: affected layers are the application host/boundary adapter (internal/app, layer 4) and surfaces/composition (cmd/gs and cmd/gitseq-mcp, layer 7). The existing Workroom fold (layer 5) contract is preserved; the adapter now exposes its existing supersede@1 declaration. Kernel encoding, signature and authority rules are unchanged. The surface contract extension needs the update described above.\n\nSecurity: the shared helper selects a fixed schema and fixed disposition rather than taking arbitrary body fields. Signing and preflight use that same helper. Ordinary retirement authority, cited-document protection and the fold's approved-head requirement remain in force. I found no additional authority, signature, secret-handling or input-bound defect in the changed code. This implementation request has no governing decision/request-chain bases, so the four-fact decision-adoption path is not applicable.\n\nSimplification: the shared supersedePayload helper is appropriate and keeps signing and preflight together. No additional abstraction is needed. A small CLI repair hint, an end-to-end MCP test and corrected commands suffice for these findings.\n\nEvidence: focused TestSupersedeAbandon tests and TestSupersedeToolOffersAbandon pass on the exact head. Compiling mutants that drop the CLI Abandon value, preflight declaration or signing declaration each make TestSupersedeAbandonRetiresARequestHoldingAnApprovedHead fail; the CLI and preflight mutants also fail TestSupersedeAbandonRefusesATargetWithNoApprovedHead. The MCP dispatch mutant survives the whole MCP suite. The documentation gate passes (50.652s), git diff --check passes, go vet of cmd/gs, cmd/gitseq-mcp and internal/app passes, and the exact-head gs build succeeds.\n\nThe broader four-package baseline test run is still in progress; no result is claimed for it here.\n\nThis is durable review evidence, not a guarded gs review verdict. The workroom has not published this head's artifact set or an addressed review request; the reporting artifact and review promise needed to bind a verdict do not yet exist. No approval or landing is authorized by this assertion.","body":{"head":"2d3b03a96f0112bbe62027e0f365e6076c3c16b7"}}